# {{displayName}}

An iOS app with [Tula Auth](https://github.com/Tula-Solutions), and the Tula API it signs in
to, which runs on your machine in Docker. The server's files are here, at the project's root;
the app is in `ios/`, a copy of the Tula repository's SwiftUI example (`examples/swiftui`).

**The Swift package is not published.** The Xcode project takes it from a checkout of the
Tula repository, by a path: `{{tulaSwiftPackage}}`, from `ios/`. That is where the checkout
was when this project was made (`--tula-repo`). If you move either, change the path in
Xcode (the project's Package Dependencies) or in `ios/TulaExample.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj`
(`relativePath`, and the two comments that repeat it). A teammate needs a checkout at the
same relative place.

**Nobody has run this project.** The example it is copied from was run in one iOS
simulator; nothing was run on a device, and no passkey sheet, Sign in with Apple sheet or
browser session has been shown by the Swift package. The Tula repository's
`examples/swiftui/README.md` says what was run.

## Start the server

```sh
bun install
bunx tula dev        # starts the stack, migrates, seeds, mints development keys
```

`tula dev` prints the URLs: the API, its reference, and Mailpit, the inbox every email the
API sends lands in (verification codes included). It writes the development keys to
`.env.local`; run it again any time, it changes nothing that is already there.

## Start the app in a simulator

Open `ios/TulaExample.xcodeproj` in Xcode, pick an iPhone simulator and run the
`TulaExample` scheme. The app asks for two things on its first screen and keeps them in its
own defaults:

- **the API's address**: `http://localhost:{{apiPort}}` (a simulator shares this machine's
  loopback address);
- **the publishable key**: the value of `TULA_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` in `.env.local`. A
  publishable key is made to ship in an app; a secret key is refused when the client is
  made, and never belongs in one.

Or give both to the scheme as the environment variables `TULA_API_URL` and
`TULA_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` (Product, Scheme, Edit Scheme, Run, Arguments). **Do not commit a
scheme or a file that holds a key**: the shared scheme here holds none.

## On a phone

A phone cannot reach `localhost`, and the app's `Info.plist` allows plain `http` to this
machine and the local network only. A passkey and an app link need `https` on a name the
phone trusts. From the project's root:

```sh
bunx tula dev --tunnel   # the stack under this machine's Tailscale Funnel address
```

It needs your own Tailscale account, signed in on this machine, and it prints the address
and the settings it did not change. Type the address into the app as the API's address.
Building onto an iPhone needs your Apple Developer team in the target's signing settings
and a bundle ID of your own. Close the tunnel with `bunx tula dev down`. Nobody has run
`tula dev --tunnel` against a phone.

## What needs your own accounts

| For | You need | Where it goes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A build on an iPhone | An Apple Developer account: a team, and a bundle ID of your own (it is `dev.tula.example.swiftui` here) | The target's Signing & Capabilities |
| Passkeys | A public `https` domain (the tunnel's will do); the Associated Domains capability with `webcredentials:<domain>`; the app registered under `nativeApps` in `tula.config.ts` with your team ID and bundle ID | Xcode; `tula.config.ts`, then `bunx tula apply` |
| Sign in with Apple | The Sign in with Apple capability for your bundle ID | Apple's developer site and Xcode; the Apple provider and the iOS app in `tula.config.ts` |
| Sign in with Google | A Google Cloud project of your own, with an iOS OAuth client ID for your bundle ID. The Swift package does not include Google's SDK: your app asks Google for the ID token | Your app; the Google provider in `tula.config.ts` |
| The tunnel | A Tailscale account | `tailscale up` on this machine |

None of these is made for you, and no identifier here is yours.

## What is here

| File | What |
| --- | --- |
| `ios/TulaExample.xcodeproj` | The Xcode project. |
| `ios/TulaExample/` | The app: where its configuration comes from, the client, the sign-in screens (`TulaAuthView`) and the account's (`TulaAccountView`). |
| `ios/TulaExampleUITests/` | The example's UI tests. **They do not run from here**: they need the Tula repository's own test fixture, which its `native/swift/fixture/ui-tests.ts` starts for `examples/swiftui`, and they fail saying so without it. Keep them as a model for tests of your own, or remove the target. |
| `compose.yaml` | The local stack: Postgres, Redis, Mailpit, the migrations and the Tula API. |
| `.env` | The stack's secrets, generated for this project: the master key, the instance admin token, the database passwords. Ignored by git. **Back up `TULA_MASTER_KEY`**: without it the stored signing keys and provider credentials cannot be opened. |
| `.env.local` | Written by `tula dev`: the API URL and the development keys. Ignored by git. |
| `.env.example` | What `.env` holds, without the values. Commit this one. |
| `tula.config.ts` | The environment's settings as code. |

## Everyday commands

```sh
bunx tula doctor          # checks the stack: database, migrations, master key, mail, Redis
bunx tula diff            # what tula.config.ts would change on the server
bunx tula apply           # make the server match tula.config.ts
bunx tula dev down        # stop the stack, and close a tunnel (the data is kept)
```

## The API image

`TULA_API_IMAGE` in `.env` names the Tula API image; it is `{{apiImage}}` here. Nothing is
published to a registry yet, so build it from the Tula repository and tag it with that name:

```sh
docker build -f apps/api/Dockerfile -t {{apiImage}} .   # in the Tula repository
```

The API is on port `{{apiPort}}` and Mailpit on `{{mailpitPort}}` (`API_PORT` and
`MAILPIT_UI_PORT` in `.env`).
